# LCD Control System with Potentiometer and Temperature Sensor using Arduino

This guide will walk you through creating an LCD control system that uses a potentiometer for brightness control and a temperature sensor (LM35) with Arduino.

## Overview

The system integrates:
- **LM35 Temperature Sensor**: Provides analog output proportional to temperature
- **LCD Display**: Shows temperature readings
- **Potentiometer**: Controls LCD brightness/contrast

## Wiring Diagram

The connections are as follows:

| Component | Pin | Arduino Pin |
|-----------|-----|-------------|
| LM35 VCC | 1 | 5V |
| LM35 GND | 2 | GND |
| LM35 Output | 3 | A0 |
| Potentiometer | - | 10K Ohm to VCC/GND |
| LCD RS | 4 | Pin 3 |
| LCD Enable | 6 | Pin 2 |
| LCD D4-D7 | 11-14 | Pins 4-7 |

## Code Implementation

```cpp
#include <LiquidCrystal.h>

// Initialize LCD with pins
LiquidCrystal lcd(2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7);

void setup() {
  lcd.begin(16, 2);
  lcd.print("Temperature:");
}

void loop() {
  int sensorValue = analogRead(A0);
  float voltage = sensorValue * (5000.0 / 1024.0);
  float temperatureC = voltage / 10.0;

  lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
  lcd.print(temperatureC);
  lcd.print(" C   ");

  delay(1000);
}
```

